--TEST--
A SQLite column reads back as the type its schema *declared* rather than as the storage class the cell arrived in — a `date`, a `time`, a `uuid`, a `decimal` and a `boolean` answer their own values, a `blob` answers the octets it is, and a cell the declaration does not describe throws — ADR 0067 §§ 6, 9
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 9's last sentence is this backend's whole type map: "SQLite has no date or
// time types; mapping keys off the *declared* column type and throws on a value
// that does not parse". Every value written below is bound as one of SQLite's
// five storage classes — `nvs_db::sqlite::encode` has no other shape to bind —
// and what each reads back as is the schema's business, not the cell's.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");

var $made = $db->execute(
    "create table t (id integer primary key, born date, opens time, ref uuid, owed decimal(10,2), live boolean, photo blob)",
    []
);
echo "created ", $made->affected(), "\n";

// Four `TEXT` cells, one `INTEGER` and one `BLOB`, and the four text ones are
// the same storage class as each other: nothing about them says which is a
// date and which is a clock reading. The blob is the other half of the map —
// `blob` is a type SQLite really has, so its cell is already § 9's answer and
// no declaration has to rescue it.
var $wrote = $db->execute(
    "insert into t (born, opens, ref, owed, live, photo) values (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)",
    [
        "2026-09-03",
        "09:30:00",
        "3f2504e0-4f89-41d3-9a0c-0305e82c3301",
        "1.25",
        1,
        "PNG" as bytes,
    ]
);
echo "inserted ", $wrote->affected(), "\n";

// § 6's typed readers ask nothing of SQLite: the column's declaration has
// already decided, so `->date()` here is the same lookup and class check it is
// against a PostgreSQL `DATE`, and `->bool()` reads a flag column the same way
// it reads a MySQL `TINYINT(1)`.
foreach ($db->query("select born, opens, ref, owed, live, photo from t", [])->all() as Core\Db\Row $row) {
    // Each reader answers `?T` because a NULL column is an ordinary absence
    // (§ 6), so the test is the one narrowing away it leaves every caller.
    var $born = $row->date("born");
    // The `TIME` row of § 9's map, which this backend can only reach through a
    // declaration: the cell is `TEXT` and every other text column beside it is
    // too.
    var $opens = $row->time("opens");
    var $ref = $row->uuid("ref");
    // A `decimal(10,2)` column has NUMERIC affinity, so SQLite has already
    // turned the bound `TEXT` into a `REAL` on the way in — the value is read
    // back for what is really stored, which is what makes the column usable.
    var $owed = $row->decimal("owed");
    var $live = $row->bool("live");
    // `BLOB`/`bytes` is the one row of the map with no text form at all
    // (`rule:types/bytes`), so it is compared as octets rather than printed.
    var $photo = $row->bytes("photo");
    var $stored = "PNG" as bytes;
    if ($born != null) {
        echo "born ", $born->format("yyyy-MM-dd"), "\n";
    }
    if ($opens != null) {
        echo "opens ", $opens->format("HH:mm:ss"), "\n";
    }
    if ($ref != null) {
        echo "ref ", $ref->toString(), "\n";
    }
    echo "owed ", $owed, "\n";
    echo "live ", $live ? "yes" : "no", "\n";
    echo "photo ", $photo == $stored ? "yes" : "no", "\n";
}

// The other half of § 9's sentence, on the same column: `20260903` is a
// perfectly good `INTEGER` and a plausible spelling of the date above, and the
// declaration is the only thing that says it is not one. It throws at the read
// rather than answering the class it happens to be stored in.
var $bad = $db->execute("insert into t (born) values (?)", ["the third"]);
echo "inserted ", $bad->affected(), "\n";
try {
    var $again = $db->query("select born from t order by id", []);
    echo "read ", $again->count(), "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "refused\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# § 3: a SQLite connection's file path comes from its config block, so
# `db.connect` covers it.
[capabilities.db]
connect = ["main"]

[db.main]
driver = "sqlite"
path = ":memory:"
--EXPECT--
created 0
inserted 1
born 2026-09-03
opens 09:30:00
ref 3f2504e0-4f89-41d3-9a0c-0305e82c3301
owed 1.25
live yes
photo yes
inserted 1
refused
